menu "Audio Recorder (P4 master output)"

choice AUDIO_RECORDER_RING_SIZE
    prompt "Master recorder PSRAM ring size"
    default AUDIO_RECORDER_RING_512K
    help
        Size of the preallocated PSRAM ring that buffers rendered master blocks
        between the audio output task and the writer task. A larger ring
        tolerates longer microSD write stalls at the cost of PSRAM. The ring is
        allocated only while recording is active.

    config AUDIO_RECORDER_RING_256K
        bool "256 KiB (~1.4 s @ 48 kHz)"
    config AUDIO_RECORDER_RING_512K
        bool "512 KiB (~2.7 s @ 48 kHz)"
    config AUDIO_RECORDER_RING_1M
        bool "1 MiB (~5.5 s @ 48 kHz)"
endchoice

config AUDIO_RECORDER_RING_BYTES
    int
    default 262144 if AUDIO_RECORDER_RING_256K
    default 524288 if AUDIO_RECORDER_RING_512K
    default 1048576 if AUDIO_RECORDER_RING_1M

config AUDIO_RECORDER_RING_INTERNAL
    bool "Allocate the recorder ring in internal RAM"
    default n
    help
        Diagnostic. Recording was measured to stall the audio output task for
        170-224 ms at a time, always inside the mixer loop, which holds no locks
        and only reads PCM out of PSRAM. Moving the ring out of PSRAM removes the
        recorder's own PSRAM traffic and tells us whether that traffic is the
        cause.

        Internal RAM is scarce (about 116 KiB free with two tracks loaded), so
        the internal ring is necessarily far smaller than the PSRAM one and
        tolerates a much shorter microSD write stall before dropping blocks.
        Dropped blocks with clean audio timing would still confirm the
        hypothesis. Do not ship this on unless the size below is proven
        sufficient.

config AUDIO_RECORDER_RING_INTERNAL_BYTES
    int "Internal recorder ring size (bytes)"
    default 32768
    depends on AUDIO_RECORDER_RING_INTERNAL
    help
        Keep well under the free internal heap: it is also needed for DMA
        buffers, task stacks and ISRs.

endmenu
